[{"ref_id":0,"title":"Field tilt: Overview","terms":"Field tilt","content":"Field tilt is the share of play in the final third that belongs to one team. Match Desk measures it as a team's share of all passes played into the attacking third: if Northgate play 30 such passes and Westmere 10, Northgate's field tilt is 75%."},{"ref_id":1,"title":"Expected threat (xT): How Match Desk uses it","terms":"Expected threat (xT)","content":"Every pass and carry is scored by the threat it adds. A pass that takes the ball from midfield into the space behind a defence adds a lot; a safe pass sideways adds almost nothing. That is how the desk decides which plays were decisive, and it powers the momentum line: each team's share of threat created over the last few minutes."},{"ref_id":2,"title":"Momentum: Momentum shifts","terms":"Momentum","content":"A momentum shift is when the line swings clearly to one side and stays there for a few minutes. It often follows a goal, a substitution, a tactical change, or a spell of pressing, and it often comes before chances."},{"ref_id":3,"title":"Field tilt: Why it matters","terms":"Field tilt","content":"Possession counts every pass, including safe ones in a team's own half. Field tilt only counts the passes that put the opponent under pressure, so it tells you which side is pinning the other back. A team can have less of the ball and still dominate territory, which is common for direct and pressing teams."}]
